                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Angel Express [Tokkyu Tenshi] is one of those overlooked Japanese indie titles that immediately wins points for personality. Developed by EasyGameStation and Starloop Studios S.L. and published by Rockin' Android, the game embraces a lighthearted premise centered on aspiring angel couriers racing to prove themselves worthy of joining an elite delivery company. The concept may sound simple, but it provides a fitting backdrop for an experience that combines platforming, racing, and party game elements into a surprisingly demanding package. Its anime-inspired visuals and energetic presentation make a strong first impression, giving the game a distinct identity that separates it from more conventional side-scrolling platformers.\n\nAt its core, Angel Express is built around speed and precision. Players navigate side-scrolling courses packed with hazards, enemies, and environmental obstacles while competing to reach the finish line as quickly as possible. The mechanics are easy to understand, with jumping, dashing, and movement forming the foundation of the gameplay, but mastering these actions is another matter entirely. Levels are designed to test reflexes and encourage experimentation, often requiring players to learn stage layouts through repetition. There is an old-school arcade mentality at work here, where failure is expected and improvement comes through practice rather than gradual hand-holding. This approach gives the game a rewarding sense of progression for dedicated players, though it can also make the learning curve feel steeper than expected.\n\nThe title truly shines when played with others. Supporting up to four players locally, Angel Express becomes a frantic competition filled with close finishes and unexpected setbacks. Racing through stages alongside friends introduces a layer of unpredictability that keeps matches entertaining, especially when everyone is trying to avoid traps while maintaining momentum. The multiplayer focus adds longevity to the experience and highlights the game's party-game influences. Even after becoming familiar with a course, the presence of other players can dramatically change how a match unfolds, turning routine runs into memorable moments of chaos.\n\nVisually, the game carries the unmistakable charm associated with many Japanese independent productions. Character designs are colorful and expressive, while the fantasy-inspired environments maintain a cheerful tone throughout the adventure. The soundtrack complements the presentation with upbeat tracks that reinforce the sense of speed and adventure. Although the graphics are modest by today's standards, the cohesive art direction helps Angel Express remain appealing years after its release. Fans of EasyGameStation's previous works will recognize the studio's knack for creating worlds that feel inviting despite their simplicity.\n\nHowever, Angel Express is not without its shortcomings. Its emphasis on memorization and precision can occasionally become frustrating, particularly during later stages where hazards leave little room for error. Players expecting a more relaxed experience may find themselves caught off guard by the game's insistence on mastery. Additionally, while the multiplayer component is enjoyable, the overall amount of content is relatively modest, meaning the game's long-term appeal largely depends on how much players enjoy replaying stages and improving their performance. Some mechanics also show their age, reflecting the game's origins as a niche title aimed at a specific audience rather than a broad mainstream market.\n\nEven with these limitations, Angel Express succeeds because it commits fully to its unique concept. It is an energetic and charming title that blends competitive racing with classic platforming in a way that few games attempt. Its combination of whimsical storytelling, demanding gameplay, and multiplayer fun makes it an interesting addition to the libraries of players who appreciate hidden gems from the indie scene. While it may not achieve the widespread recognition of EasyGameStation's more celebrated projects, it remains an enjoyable experience for those willing to embrace its challenges and quirky personality.\n\nRating: 6/10",
